export function parseRange(text) {
  const [from, to] = text.split("-");
  const fromNum = Number(from);
  const toNum = to === undefined ? fromNum : Number(to);
  return { from: fromNum, to: toNum };
}

response.text:
The issue was that `parseRange` did not handle single-value range strings (e.g., `"7"`). In such cases, `text.split("-")` resulted in an array with only one element, leaving the `to` variable as `undefined`. Calling `Number(undefined)` returns `NaN`, causing the test to fail.

I have updated `parse-range.mjs` to check if `to` is `undefined` and, if so, set it to the same value as `from`.

```javascript
export function parseRange(text) {
  const [from, to] = text.split("-");
  const fromNum = Number(from);
  const toNum = to === undefined ? fromNum : Number(to);
  return { from: fromNum, to: toNum };
}
```
